# Bilingual responses (Thai 60% / English 40%)

Every reply to the user must mix **Thai and English** in roughly this split:

| Language | Share | Role |
|----------|-------|------|
| **ไทย** | ~60% | Main explanation, steps, conclusions, recommendations |
| **English** | ~40% | Technical terms, commands, code comments, short labels, key nouns |

## How to apply

- **Default structure:** Thai paragraphs first; English for identifiers (`git push`, `SKILL.md`, error messages, API names).
- **Do not** write two full duplicate versions (not a full Thai block then a full English block).
- **Do not** alternate every sentence TH/EN unless it stays readable.
- **Code blocks:** commands and code stay as-is (usually English). Explain surrounding text in Thai.
- **Tables/headers:** Either language is fine; body cells favor Thai with English terms where precise.
- **Skills invoked** (`/debug`, `/git-push`): follow the skill workflow; still keep the 60/40 voice in user-facing prose.
- **Skill `## SKILL REPORT`:** use English section labels from [`templates/template.skill-report.md`](../templates/template.skill-report.md); body prose Thai ~60%. Do **not** write two full duplicate versions of the same content in Thai and English.

## Examples

**Good (≈60/40):**
> ตอนนี้ branch `main` ตรงกับ `origin` แล้ว แต่ยังมีไฟล์ค้างใน working tree — ต้อง **commit** ก่อนถึงจะ push ขึ้น GitHub ได้  
> Run `git status` to see unstaged paths.

**Avoid:**
> Full reply only in English.  
> Full reply only in Thai when the user needs English technical labels.  
> 100% Thai + 100% English duplicate sections.

## Exceptions

- User asks for **one language only** → follow that request for that turn.
- Quoted logs, stderr, or UI strings → keep original language.
- Local notes under `vault/` (if any): user-owned; not part of pack behavior.
- Task-specific skills (`/debug`, `/git-push`, …) override voice style for workflow steps; keep the user-facing summary bilingual.
